  "estimatedMinutes": 2,
  "statement": "Let \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) be the roots of the equation \\(x^2-mx+2=0.\\) Suppose that \\(a+\\tfrac1b\\) and \\(b+\\tfrac1a\\) are the roots of the equation \\(x^2-px+q=0.\\) What is \\(q?\\)",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{5}{2}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{7}{2}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(4\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{9}{2}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "\\(8\\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "d",
    "value": "9/2"
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.amc-10b-2006-p14.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Since \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are roots of \\(x^2-mx+2,\\) we have \\(ab=2.\\)\n\nThe value \\(q\\) is the product of the new roots: \\[ \\begin{aligned} q&=\\left(a+\\tfrac1b\\right)\\left(b+\\tfrac1a\\right)\\\\ &=ab+1+1+\\tfrac{1}{ab}\\\\ &=2+2+\\tfrac12=\\tfrac92. \\end{aligned} \\]\n\nThus, the correct answer is D.",
      "hints": [
        "By Vieta’s formulas, \\(ab=2\\)",
        "\\(q\\) is the product \\(\\left(a+\\tfrac1b\\right)\\left(b+\\tfrac1a\\right)\\)"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
